              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stipulor, stipulārī — \"to bargain for, to demand a formal promise\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Stipulātus est ut pecūnia redderētur. He stipulated that the money be returned."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: stipulor, stipulārī, stipulātus sum. In Roman law the stipulātiō was a contract made by question and answer. English stipulate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The origin of stipulor is not settled: Lewis & Short give no sure account, and EtymDB has no parent for it. English stipulate comes from it."
            }
          ]
        },
